          "title": "You'll want to know: détacher",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "détacher — \"to remove a stain from; to untie\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Le pressing a bien détaché ma chemise blanche. The dry cleaner got the stain out of my white shirt."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two senses: from tache 'stain', and from attacher — détacher sa ceinture, to undo one's belt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "détacher in the sense 'to untie' comes from Old French destachier (EtymDB), the opposite of attacher; in the sense 'to remove a stain' it is built on tache 'stain'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
